Alex: And Nvidia stops shipping AI chips to China.

16
00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:50,940
Alex: Plus thirteen more.

17
00:00:50,940 --> 00:00:51,700
Alex: Let's get into it.

18
00:00:53,220 --> 00:00:54,880
Maya: Time for The Ticker.

19
00:00:54,880 --> 00:01:01,920
Maya: At Wednesday's close, the S&P 500 finished at 7,324, down 0.2 percent.

20
00:01:01,920 --> 00:01:08,910
Maya: The Nasdaq Composite was up 0.4 percent and the Dow was down 1.1 percent.

21
00:01:09,370 --> 00:01:13,660
Alex: And in big tech, Amazon had the best day, up 3.8 percent,

22
00:01:13,660 --> 00:01:18,050
Alex: while Nvidia was at the other end, down 2.9 percent.

23
00:01:18,050 --> 00:01:19,760
Alex: That's a snapshot, not advice.

24
00:01:21,440 --> 00:01:26,520
Alex: Musk just said firing Jensen Huang feels like kicking Michael Jordan off a winning

25
00:01:26,520 --> 00:01:28,560
Alex: team, according to TechCrunch.

26
00:01:29,530 --> 00:01:33,800
Maya: He signed a petition with 200,000 people to get Nvidia back.

27
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:34,510
Alex: Seriously?

28
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:37,120
Maya: Nvidia’s board says they’re not planning a return.

29
00:01:37,600 --> 00:01:39,660
Alex: So Musk thinks no one can replace him.

30
00:01:40,070 --> 00:01:42,660
Maya: He talks about loyalty and tech knowledge,

31
00:01:42,660 --> 00:01:45,560
Maya: but Nvidia’s interim CEO is already in place.

32
00:01:46,110 --> 00:01:50,160
Alex: And the board’s focus is on the company’s continuity, not the hype.

33
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:53,900
Maya: That’s the headline, now let’s dig into the market angle.

34
00:01:54,500 --> 00:01:57,580
Maya: What’s the real depth behind Musk’s “Michael Jordan” line?

35
00:01:58,040 --> 00:02:03,050
Alex: He’s basically saying Jensen’s tech know‑how and team loyalty are irreplaceable.

36
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:05,240
Maya: So he’s framing it like a sports team?

37
00:02:05,550 --> 00:02:10,780
Alex: Yeah, and he pointed out a lot of Nvidia staff have higher offers but stay because

38
00:02:10,780 --> 00:02:11,320
Alex: of Jensen.

39
00:02:11,320 --> 00:02:12,520
Maya: Did anyone else weigh in?

40
00:02:12,780 --> 00:02:18,080
Alex: Palmer Luckey jumped in, saying China wants Jensen out and that hurts Nvidia’s

41
00:02:18,080 --> 00:02:18,560
Alex: valuation.

42
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:19,700
Maya: Interesting angle.

43
00:02:20,150 --> 00:02:23,500
Alex: And the board’s stance is clear—no comeback plan,

44
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:25,780
Alex: just moving forward with the interim CEO.

45
00:02:26,670 --> 00:02:32,280
Alex: I think Musk is just throwing hype, calling a CEO a Michael Jordan to stir drama.

46
00:02:32,825 --> 00:02:33,210
Alex: Seriously?

47
00:02:33,440 --> 00:02:36,880
Maya: It shows a chunk of the community feels the board's move is risky.

48
00:02:37,370 --> 00:02:41,320
Alex: But the board says no comeback plan, so the risk is only in perception.

49
00:02:42,060 --> 00:02:46,270
Maya: True, yet the interim CEO has a solid Microsoft background,

50
00:02:46,270 --> 00:02:47,640
Maya: which could stabilize the market.

51
00:02:48,280 --> 00:02:53,120
Alex: Still, the loyalty angle—staff staying against higher offers—could hint at deeper

52
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:53,600
Alex: unrest.

53
00:02:54,300 --> 00:02:58,820
Maya: It sounds like Nvidia is balancing internal morale with external investor

54
00:02:58,820 --> 00:02:59,460
Maya: confidence.

55
00:03:01,440 --> 00:03:07,070
Maya: 15-0 Republican vote, Democrats absent, that’s the kicker, Ars Technica reports.

56
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:10,150
Alex: So Cruz just ran the show without a quorum.

57
00:03:10,580 --> 00:03:13,320
Maya: He said they’d do their job even if the others refuse.

58
00:03:13,640 --> 00:03:14,140
Alex: That’s wild.

59
00:03:14,750 --> 00:03:20,260
Maya: The nominee is Danielle Thumann, a former legal advisor to FCC chair Brendan Carr.

60
00:03:21,120 --> 00:03:24,080
Alex: She supposedly gave false testimony about that role.

61
00:03:24,430 --> 00:03:28,300
Maya: Republicans hope to confirm her before Senate flips in January.

62
00:03:28,300 --> 00:03:29,020
Alex: Makes sense.

63
00:03:29,550 --> 00:03:31,060
Maya: And that’s all we know from the article.

64
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:35,040
Alex: What about the actual vetting process that went on before this vote?

65
00:03:35,300 --> 00:03:39,920
Maya: The committee reviews the nominee’s background, then votes; if they pass,

66
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:41,520
Maya: the full Senate needs a majority.

67
00:03:42,060 --> 00:03:43,860
Alex: So they cut straight to the final step?

68
00:03:44,280 --> 00:03:48,630
Maya: Yeah, they skipped the usual committee debate because the Democrats didn't show up.

69
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:50,680
Alex: Did anyone try to stop the quorum?

70
00:03:51,115 --> 00:03:54,420
Maya: The Democrats said they’d prevent a quorum, but they stayed away.

71
00:03:54,840 --> 00:03:56,780
Alex: How does that affect the overall confirmation?

72
00:03:57,310 --> 00:04:00,580
Maya: Without a quorum, the committee can still move forward,

73
00:04:00,580 --> 00:04:02,820
Maya: but the full Senate still has to approve later.

74
00:04:03,580 --> 00:04:06,460
Maya: This feels like a political stunt, not a real vetting.

75
00:04:06,970 --> 00:04:08,580
Alex: But the committee still did its job.

76
00:04:09,260 --> 00:04:12,080
Maya: So the real question is, what happens in the full Senate?

77
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:15,500
Alex: Honestly, it's just a formality if they get a quorum.

78
00:04:15,930 --> 00:04:19,620
Maya: I think it signals that the commission can ignore dissenting voices.

79
00:04:19,860 --> 00:04:23,410
Alex: And that the confirmation might slip through before the power shift.

80
00:04:24,320 --> 00:04:25,470
Maya: That’s the point, right?

81
00:04:25,470 --> 00:04:26,220
Maya: They’re moving fast.

82
00:04:26,860 --> 00:04:28,900
Alex: I guess that’s the reality of the process.

83
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:32,540
Alex: Maya, what did Ukraine do to that bridge?

84
00:04:32,970 --> 00:04:36,320
Maya: They hit a steel arch with missiles and drones, no Starlink.

85
00:04:36,740 --> 00:04:37,820
Alex: No Starlink?

86
00:04:37,820 --> 00:04:38,960
Alex: How did they guide the shots?

87
00:04:39,460 --> 00:04:42,420
Maya: They used mesh‑radio drones as a relay network.

88
00:04:42,420 --> 00:04:43,200
Alex: Huh, that's wild.

89
00:04:43,760 --> 00:04:46,940
Maya: Russian air defenses were reportedly overwhelmed first.

90
00:04:47,160 --> 00:04:48,380
Alex: So the drones set the stage?

91
00:04:48,840 --> 00:04:51,710
Maya: Yes, then the Flamingo cruise missile finished the job.

92
00:04:52,030 --> 00:04:57,750
Alex: It was a coordinated night attack, November 15‑16, Ars Technica reports.

93
00:04:58,100 --> 00:05:00,640
Maya: What exactly does that mesh‑radio tech do?

94
00:05:00,970 --> 00:05:03,860
Alex: Think of each drone as a hop‑scotch board,

95
00:05:03,860 --> 00:05:07,200
Alex: passing signals to the missile until it finally reaches the target.

96
00:05:07,560 --> 00:05:10,360
Maya: So the drones themselves are the link, not the operator?

97
00:05:10,780 --> 00:05:15,980
Alex: Right, the human stays off the line; the relay chain keeps the missile fed until it

98
00:05:15,980 --> 00:05:16,670
Alex: hits the arch.

99
00:05:17,340 --> 00:05:20,420
Maya: And Russia started using the same trick on Ukrainian cities?

100
00:05:20,900 --> 00:05:24,260
Alex: Yeah, they reversed the play, turning the technology against them.

101
00:05:24,320 --> 00:05:25,420
Maya: Who’s saying this, exactly?

102
00:05:25,980 --> 00:05:28,510
Alex: The Financial Times broke it down,

103
00:05:28,510 --> 00:05:32,880
Alex: citing how Ukrainian forces adapted the Russian battlefield innovation.

104
00:05:33,340 --> 00:05:35,500
Alex: That relay trick flips the battlefield, huh.

105
00:05:35,850 --> 00:05:38,740
Maya: But it also shows how tech can be turned on its own side.

106
00:05:39,120 --> 00:05:43,200
Alex: The drones are like a chain—no human in the middle, just packets hopping.

107
00:05:43,830 --> 00:05:46,520
Maya: So the same method Russia later used on cities?

108
00:05:46,900 --> 00:05:51,720
Alex: It suggests a new layer of vulnerability, but no one says what else could break.

109
00:05:52,400 --> 00:05:56,960
Maya: In any case, the cost of buying that tech or defending against it is up in the air.

110
00:05:57,450 --> 00:05:59,860
Alex: And the question is, who pays for the next wave?

111
00:06:00,230 --> 00:06:03,000
Maya: The market will decide, and the price tag will follow.

112
00:06:04,920 --> 00:06:09,520
Maya: Microsoft’s Nadella says AI agents will rework how we pay for web traffic and

113
00:06:09,520 --> 00:06:11,840
Maya: transactions, according to TechCrunch.

114
00:06:12,380 --> 00:06:13,770
Alex: That sounds like a big shift.

115
00:06:14,300 --> 00:06:16,540
Maya: He compared it to when Google overtook Yahoo,

116
00:06:16,540 --> 00:06:19,780
Maya: and said the current ad‑based models will get reshuffled.

117
00:06:20,270 --> 00:06:21,580
Alex: So it’s a cost‑per‑click push?

118
00:06:22,060 --> 00:06:26,160
Maya: He added that if you don’t embrace agents, someone else will.

119
00:06:26,160 --> 00:06:29,440
Maya: Amazon’s block on agents might open the door for competitors.

120
00:06:30,240 --> 00:06:32,840
Alex: Shopify could leap ahead if it lets agents in.

121
00:06:33,320 --> 00:06:37,380
Maya: That’s the crux—companies that lock out agents risk losing market share.

122
00:06:37,850 --> 00:06:42,720
Alex: It’s a gamble, but the tech‑side view is that agents could change the economics of

123
00:06:42,720 --> 00:06:43,120
Alex: the web.

124
00:06:43,550 --> 00:06:45,420
Alex: What exactly did he mean by “agent”?

125
00:06:45,870 --> 00:06:50,610
Maya: He was talking about software agents that can browse the web on behalf of users,

126
00:06:50,610 --> 00:06:53,500
Maya: pulling data and making purchases automatically.

127
00:06:53,720 --> 00:06:55,020
Alex: So like a virtual shopper?

128
00:06:55,540 --> 00:07:00,200
Maya: Yeah, but the point is they change who pays for traffic—shifting from eyeball ads

129
00:07:00,200 --> 00:07:02,540
Maya: to cost‑per‑click or transaction fees.

130
00:07:02,740 --> 00:07:04,220
Alex: He said Amazon blocks them?

131
00:07:04,560 --> 00:07:08,800
Maya: Yes, Amazon blocks agents from its site, so if they stay shut,

132
00:07:08,800 --> 00:07:12,910
Maya: a platform like Shopify could open its doors and attract those agents,

133
00:07:12,910 --> 00:07:13,760
Maya: gaining a foothold.

134
00:07:13,900 --> 00:07:14,480
Alex: Is that all?

135
00:07:14,840 --> 00:07:19,540
Maya: He added that if a company resists agents, someone else will win the race,

136
00:07:19,540 --> 00:07:23,710
Maya: just like Google overtook Yahoo when the web moved from portals to search.

137
00:07:24,350 --> 00:07:27,080
Maya: If Amazon keeps locking out agents,

138
00:07:27,080 --> 00:07:30,340
Maya: it’s a risk that Shopify could leap ahead by opening the door.

139
00:07:30,813 --> 00:07:35,900
Alex: But the tech side worries: agents could break the current ad models before anyone

140
00:07:35,900 --> 00:07:36,480
Alex: profits.

141
00:07:37,150 --> 00:07:40,780
Maya: Do you think the shift will really favor transaction fees over eyeballs?

142
00:07:41,160 --> 00:07:45,900
Alex: Maybe, but the first wave of agents might still be messy and buggy—hard to trust

143
00:07:45,900 --> 00:07:46,540
Alex: them to pay.

144
00:07:47,090 --> 00:07:50,720
Maya: Hold on, didn’t Nadella say the agents would change who pays for traffic?

145
00:07:51,130 --> 00:07:56,120
Alex: It sounds like a new game, but only if the agents actually work reliably; otherwise

146
00:07:56,120 --> 00:07:57,290
Alex: the old model sticks.

147
00:07:57,880 --> 00:08:02,800
Maya: So the real gamble is whether platforms will let the agents in or block them and

148
00:08:02,800 --> 00:08:03,420
Maya: lose ground.

149
00:08:04,020 --> 00:08:08,120
Alex: In the end, it’s a test of whether the web’s economics can adapt to automated

150
00:08:08,120 --> 00:08:08,660
Alex: shoppers.

151
00:08:10,400 --> 00:08:12,200
Alex: That’s a big pivot, huh?

152
00:08:12,200 --> 00:08:17,140
Alex: Nvidia just cut off H200 shipments after getting clearance.

153
00:08:17,140 --> 00:08:22,080
Alex: The interim CEO said they’re not shipping licensed Hopper data center products to

154
00:08:22,080 --> 00:08:22,500
Alex: China.

155
00:08:22,940 --> 00:08:23,690
Maya: No way.

156
00:08:23,690 --> 00:08:25,000
Maya: They had approval last December.

157
00:08:25,570 --> 00:08:29,300
Alex: The Verge says Colette Kress made the call on earnings.

158
00:08:29,300 --> 00:08:32,320
Alex: She didn’t explain why or if they’ll touch China later.

159
00:08:32,740 --> 00:08:34,510
Maya: I think it’s a political move.

160
00:08:34,510 --> 00:08:37,120
Maya: The article doesn’t say, but the timing matters.

161
00:08:37,660 --> 00:08:40,020
Alex: Funny, they fought for it, then backpedal.

162
00:08:40,020 --> 00:08:40,840
Alex: What’s the next step?

163
00:08:41,210 --> 00:08:44,880
Maya: Might mean no more sales, but could keep other business open.

164
00:08:44,880 --> 00:08:46,160
Maya: The article stops short.

165
00:08:46,730 --> 00:08:50,360
Alex: So they’re pulling back on the big chips, but nothing on other products.

166
00:08:50,600 --> 00:08:51,440
Maya: Right, that’s the take.

167
00:08:51,800 --> 00:08:52,700
Maya: What’s the funding angle?

168
00:08:53,050 --> 00:08:56,740
Alex: They’re not shipping any licensed Hopper data‑center chips,

169
00:08:56,740 --> 00:08:59,180
Alex: so the revenue stream from China is gone.

170
00:08:59,380 --> 00:08:59,780
Maya: No way.

171
00:09:00,300 --> 00:09:04,640
Alex: The article says Colette Kress made that call on the earnings call,

172
00:09:04,640 --> 00:09:07,040
Alex: but didn’t explain why she stopped the shipments.

173
00:09:07,280 --> 00:09:07,940
Maya: So it’s a mystery?

174
00:09:08,460 --> 00:09:11,410
Alex: The white house had given clearance last December,

175
00:09:11,410 --> 00:09:13,770
Alex: so the shutdown is a deliberate pullback.

176
00:09:13,860 --> 00:09:15,200
Maya: Any hint on future deals?

177
00:09:15,660 --> 00:09:19,920
Alex: No mention of other business, just that the H200 shipments are halted.

178
00:09:20,300 --> 00:09:21,400
Alex: That’s a big pivot, huh?

179
00:09:21,480 --> 00:09:22,000
Maya: No way.

180
00:09:22,510 --> 00:09:24,690
Maya: Hold on, you’re reading politics into it.

181
00:09:24,860 --> 00:09:25,460
Maya: That’s the take.

182
00:09:26,140 --> 00:09:28,400
Alex: I’m curious if they’ll keep other business open.

183
00:09:28,910 --> 00:09:29,680
Maya: They might.

184
00:09:29,680 --> 00:09:30,640
Maya: That’s the bottom line.

185
00:09:32,420 --> 00:09:35,160
Maya: Nvidia’s H200 chips out of China, all done.

186
00:09:35,760 --> 00:09:37,440
Alex: Wait, how did they even get a loophole?

187
00:09:37,910 --> 00:09:39,560
Maya: Huang brokered a U.S.

188
00:09:39,560 --> 00:09:42,640
Maya: route so China could pay a 25% tariff.

189
00:09:42,800 --> 00:09:43,960
Alex: That’s a weird compromise.

190
00:09:44,670 --> 00:09:47,700
Maya: The board’s new committee just formed to watch export rules.

191
00:09:48,260 --> 00:09:51,800
Alex: So no more H200s, but top‑line stays the same?

192
00:09:52,150 --> 00:09:57,480
Maya: Jefferies says revenue could drop $44‑$62 billion next year.

193
00:09:57,950 --> 00:10:01,040
Alex: But they claim backlog of demand keeps revenue flat.

194
00:10:01,840 --> 00:10:06,580
Maya: Chinese labs still training on Grace Blackwell chips, but sales to them now barred.

195
00:10:07,180 --> 00:10:10,700
Alex: What’s the deal with that export‑control guy Dan Fisher‑Owens?

196
00:10:11,190 --> 00:10:15,960
Maya: He told us that any data center wanting Nvidia chips must prove it isn’t serving

197
00:10:15,960 --> 00:10:18,980
Maya: Chinese customers; otherwise they’ll be blocked.

198
00:10:19,500 --> 00:10:21,760
Alex: So they’re actually going to audit every buyer?

199
00:10:22,020 --> 00:10:25,870
Maya: That’s the idea—certification of non‑China service,

200
00:10:25,870 --> 00:10:30,420
Maya: or they lose access to the H200 and even the Grace Blackwell line.

201
00:10:30,560 --> 00:10:31,000
Alex: “Says who?”

202
00:10:31,450 --> 00:10:35,860
Maya: The attorney’s comment came from a TechCrunch interview; it’s a standard compliance

203
00:10:35,860 --> 00:10:37,180
Maya: check under the new rules.

204
00:10:37,720 --> 00:10:39,360
Alex: Sounds like a bureaucratic maze.

205
00:10:39,910 --> 00:10:44,800
Maya: Plus, the board’s new committee is overseeing national‑security, export‑control,

206
00:10:44,800 --> 00:10:47,800
Maya: and global‑trade risks, so they’re tightening the screws.

207
00:10:48,580 --> 00:10:53,480
Alex: Meaning Nvidia’s still keeping the top‑line, but it’s a different customer base now.

208
00:10:54,100 --> 00:10:57,260
Maya: I think they’re just trimming a risky line but keeping the cash flow.

209
00:10:57,720 --> 00:11:00,720
Alex: That sounds like a band‑aid, not a real solution.

210
00:11:01,080 --> 00:11:04,900
Maya: The board’s committee shows they’re tightening controls, not cutting corners.

211
00:11:05,420 --> 00:11:05,900
Alex: Tightening?

212
00:11:05,900 --> 00:11:08,240
Alex: Sounds more like a bureaucratic nightmare.

213
00:11:08,640 --> 00:11:12,300
Maya: The backlog of demand could keep revenue flat, even if China’s out.

214
00:11:12,750 --> 00:11:14,680
Alex: But who’s buying those chips now?

215
00:11:14,680 --> 00:11:15,800
Alex: The market is shrinking.

216
00:11:16,680 --> 00:11:20,280
Maya: Maybe it’s a pivot to other regions—Singapore, Malaysia, Thailand.

217
00:11:21,220 --> 00:11:25,400
Alex: If they can’t certify non‑China use, they’ll lose those customers,

218
00:11:25,400 --> 00:11:26,520
Alex: so it’s a real hit.

219
00:11:28,060 --> 00:11:35,860
Alex: Nvidia’s private stake is now $65.5 billion, up from $22 billion last January.

220
00:11:36,330 --> 00:11:38,060
Maya: That’s a jump of almost three times.

221
00:11:38,060 --> 00:11:38,504
Alex: Huh.

222
00:11:38,504 --> 00:11:39,400
Maya: Who’s driving that?

223
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:43,980
Alex: Jensen Huang pushed a $16 billion equity spree last quarter.

224
00:11:44,054 --> 00:11:45,070
Maya: And she’s stepping out?

225
00:11:45,460 --> 00:11:49,440
Alex: Interim CEO plus a new permanent leader could cool the fire.

226
00:11:49,830 --> 00:11:51,540
Maya: So the VC pipeline might shrink.

227
00:11:51,870 --> 00:11:52,760
Maya: What about the guarantees?

228
00:11:53,320 --> 00:11:57,800
Alex: They poured $118 billion into guarantees,

229
00:11:57,800 --> 00:12:02,380
Alex: $105 billion of that for OpenAI’s Ohio center.

230
00:12:02,520 --> 00:12:03,440
Maya: That’s a lot of cash.

231
00:12:03,990 --> 00:12:05,680
Alex: Yeah, it feels like a safety net.

232
00:12:06,160 --> 00:12:07,180
Maya: Who’s saying this might slow?

233
00:12:07,580 --> 00:12:12,310
Alex: Kress warned Huang’s exit could make investors go more cautious, smaller checks.

234
00:12:13,260 --> 00:12:14,520
Maya: So the pipeline might shrink.

235
00:12:14,920 --> 00:12:17,790
Alex: And the 350 startup count stays the same?

236
00:12:18,150 --> 00:12:23,010
Maya: They’ve backed almost 350, but no new round numbers after the spike.

237
00:12:23,490 --> 00:12:25,630
Alex: Sounds like a firework, but only a flicker.

238
00:12:26,020 --> 00:12:27,580
Maya: Feels like a sprint that might stall.

239
00:12:27,595 --> 00:12:27,900
Alex: Huh.

240
00:12:28,380 --> 00:12:30,720
Maya: If the checks shrink, the pipeline will thin.

241
00:12:31,280 --> 00:12:33,960
Alex: Right, but they still have 350 names.

242
00:12:34,360 --> 00:12:37,420
Maya: But without the big pushes, those names might not grow.

243
00:12:37,830 --> 00:12:40,490
Alex: Exactly, and the guarantees keep the heat alive.

244
00:12:40,960 --> 00:12:44,060
Maya: It seems the next CEO will have to balance fire and safety.

245
00:12:45,720 --> 00:12:49,320
Maya: Hey Alex, any idea why Anthropic shot its price to $83?

246
00:12:50,140 --> 00:12:54,800
Alex: They pushed the offer up, selling 980 million shares,

247
00:12:54,800 --> 00:12:57,640
Alex: not the 825 million they’d planned.

248
00:12:58,130 --> 00:12:59,820
Maya: That’s more than the range they said, right?

249
00:12:59,960 --> 00:13:03,120
Alex: Yeah, over the $70‑$78 band.

250
00:13:03,720 --> 00:13:08,880
Maya: And they’re pulling in about $81.3 billion, beating SpaceX’s record.

251
00:13:09,640 --> 00:13:11,530
Alex: 81.3 billion, huh?

252
00:13:11,530 --> 00:13:13,140
Alex: That’s a lot of cash for a startup.

253
00:13:13,730 --> 00:13:19,440
Maya: Plus, Amazon, Google, Microsoft, Nvidia, and AMD are all in the mix,

254
00:13:19,440 --> 00:13:21,140
Maya: each investing $26 billion.

255
00:13:22,530 --> 00:13:24,380
Alex: So the big players back them—makes sense.

256
00:13:25,010 --> 00:13:29,240
Alex: What about the tech side—how does Claude actually pile up that revenue bump?

257
00:13:29,700 --> 00:13:34,800
Maya: Claude Code drove 1,200% jump in the last nine months,

258
00:13:34,800 --> 00:13:37,120
Maya: and that’s the tool customers love for coding.

259
00:13:37,590 --> 00:13:41,900
Maya: Right, the run‑rate revenue is about $81 billion, not a trillion.

260
00:13:41,900 --> 00:13:42,280
Alex: That’s huge.

261
00:13:42,920 --> 00:13:46,220
Maya: Each co‑founder sold 3.38 million shares,

262
00:13:46,220 --> 00:13:51,160
Maya: netting $1.96 billion—shows insiders backing the price.

263
00:13:51,680 --> 00:13:54,980
Alex: Funny how the founders cash out while the company goes public.

264
00:13:55,490 --> 00:13:59,440
Maya: And the big techs—Amazon, Google, Microsoft, Nvidia,

265
00:13:59,440 --> 00:14:02,940
Maya: AMD—each poured $26 billion into the IPO.

266
00:14:03,380 --> 00:14:03,840
Maya: That's wild.

267
00:14:04,731 --> 00:14:08,060
Alex: But the price hike just means they over‑estimated demand.

268
00:14:08,600 --> 00:14:13,300
Maya: The $26 billion from each big tech feels like a vote of confidence, though.

269
00:14:13,690 --> 00:14:17,920
Alex: Might just be a hedge; they’re buying into a future that might not deliver.

270
00:14:18,420 --> 00:14:24,090
Maya: The 1,200% revenue jump on Claude Code shows customers love it,

271
00:14:24,090 --> 00:14:25,380
Maya: so demand could be real.

272
00:14:26,040 --> 00:14:31,900
Alex: Still, the $104 billion loss in nine months is a red flag about sustainability.

273
00:14:32,470 --> 00:14:37,200
Maya: If the founders are cashing out, maybe they see a quick exit, not a long‑term play.

274
00:14:37,680 --> 00:14:41,730
Alex: I think the real test is whether the tech holds up when the market tightens.

275
00:14:43,380 --> 00:14:46,710
Alex: Anthropic is about to go public, and people are lining up.

276
00:14:47,280 --> 00:14:49,760
Maya: According to CNBC, that’s the headline.

277
00:14:50,230 --> 00:14:53,000
Alex: The hype is real, but the mechanics are still murky.

278
00:14:53,510 --> 00:14:58,240
Maya: Retail could grab up to 20% of the 825 million shares.

279
00:14:58,880 --> 00:15:03,760
Alex: Dan Shetty drained his Roth IRA for $12,000 in hopes of a stake.

280
00:15:04,270 --> 00:15:09,780
Maya: Vanda Research projects $250 million of retail net buying—double SpaceX.

281
00:15:10,470 --> 00:15:16,920
Alex: The IPO price range is $70–$78, but sources say it might go higher.

282
00:15:17,440 --> 00:15:21,470
Maya: Joe Pak warns allocations are tight and macro conditions are tough.

283
00:15:21,935 --> 00:15:24,900
Maya: What’s still nagging people after the basics hit the airwaves?

284
00:15:25,320 --> 00:15:26,660
Alex: No way, the numbers are wild.

285
00:15:27,630 --> 00:15:29,260
Maya: How big is the company actually?

286
00:15:29,680 --> 00:15:36,320
Alex: It’s less than six years old, but it’s already pitching a $1.405 trillion valuation

287
00:15:36,320 --> 00:15:39,780
Alex: and wants to lift $64.4 billion.

288
00:15:40,520 --> 00:15:41,180
Maya: That’s a monster.

289
00:15:41,800 --> 00:15:49,320
Alex: If the demand hits, it could pull in more than SpaceX’s $85.7 billion IPO; that’s

290
00:15:49,320 --> 00:15:51,020
Alex: the only other comparable beast.

291
00:15:51,260 --> 00:15:52,280
Maya: So the hype could be real.

292
00:15:52,790 --> 00:15:55,900
Alex: I think the hype is mostly about the numbers, not the tech.

293
00:15:56,450 --> 00:15:59,280
Maya: It’s the retail pull, that’s the story for the market.

294
00:15:59,280 --> 00:16:01,000
Alex: No way, 20% allocation?

295
00:16:01,430 --> 00:16:04,080
Maya: That’s a lot of demand, but allocations stay tight.

296
00:16:04,700 --> 00:16:07,690
Alex: If it eclipses SpaceX, the valuation could jump.

297
00:16:08,420 --> 00:16:10,360
Maya: But the price might still be above the range.

298
00:16:10,980 --> 00:16:12,980
Alex: So it’s a gamble for the average investor.

299
00:16:13,400 --> 00:16:16,340
Maya: Precisely, the upside is huge, the risk is real.

300
00:16:17,960 --> 00:16:20,860
Alex: You're listening to First Tick, from Bits and Tickers.

301
00:16:20,860 --> 00:16:22,520
Alex: That's about the halfway mark.

302
00:16:22,520 --> 00:16:23,800
Alex: Still to come.

303
00:16:23,800 --> 00:16:29,090
Alex: Palo Alto inks $4.5 billion deal for AI startup Applied Compute,

304
00:16:29,090 --> 00:16:31,630
Alex: shares pop 7% on earnings.

305
00:16:31,630 --> 00:16:34,620
Alex: Microsoft backs Nvidia’s interim boss.

306
00:16:34,620 --> 00:16:41,040
Alex: And India’s Humyn Labs raises $47M to feed US robotics companies the data they need.

307
00:16:41,640 --> 00:16:44,910
Maya: And if you just joined us, I'm Maya, that's Alex,

308
00:16:44,910 --> 00:16:47,040
Maya: and every story is linked in the show notes.

309
00:16:48,670 --> 00:16:53,340
Maya: Hot deal, Palo Alto just snapped up Applied Compute for $4.5 billion.

310
00:16:54,270 --> 00:16:58,440
Alex: The startup helps run custom AI models, not just security.

311
00:16:59,360 --> 00:17:02,120
Maya: They say it could dwarf their own cybersecurity business.

312
00:17:02,120 --> 00:17:02,800
Alex: Makes sense.

313
00:17:03,470 --> 00:17:06,920
Maya: Shares jumped 7% after the earnings beat and the deal.

314
00:17:07,300 --> 00:17:14,690
Alex: Quarterly ARR for next‑gen security hit $9.73 billion, up 66%.

315
00:17:15,290 --> 00:17:23,000
Maya: CEO Nikesh Arora pushed revenue guidance up to $14.52‑$14.57 billion.

316
00:17:23,940 --> 00:17:27,880
Alex: He also warned about tens of thousands of virtual patches needed.

317
00:17:28,270 --> 00:17:32,060
Maya: Looks like AI is turning into a new revenue stream for cyber firms.

318
00:17:32,520 --> 00:17:35,380
Alex: What exactly does Applied Compute do for the models?

319
00:17:35,790 --> 00:17:39,310
Maya: They help companies run and tweak their own AI models,

320
00:17:39,310 --> 00:17:41,980
Maya: keeping data on‑prem or in private clouds.

321
00:17:42,540 --> 00:17:44,160
Alex: Why would a cyber firm buy that?

322
00:17:44,380 --> 00:17:48,780
Maya: The CEO said it could be “bigger than what we do” in security—like a new revenue

323
00:17:48,780 --> 00:17:49,260
Maya: engine.

324
00:17:49,260 --> 00:17:49,840
Alex: Makes sense.

325
00:17:50,510 --> 00:17:53,800
Maya: And it’s all‑stock for $4.5 billion,

326
00:17:53,800 --> 00:17:57,700
Maya: so Palo Alto keeps cash flow intact while adding AI chops.

327
00:17:58,300 --> 00:17:59,880
Alex: How much patching are we looking at?

328
00:18:00,220 --> 00:18:03,560
Maya: Tens of thousands of virtual patches by year‑end,

329
00:18:03,560 --> 00:18:05,640
Maya: each taking three to nine months to implement.

330
00:18:06,190 --> 00:18:07,780
Maya: This could be a game‑changer for them.

331
00:18:08,180 --> 00:18:11,320
Alex: Maybe, but only if the AI side actually delivers.

332
00:18:12,330 --> 00:18:16,780
Maya: They’re betting that custom AI models will outpace their current security revenue.

333
00:18:17,280 --> 00:18:21,520
Alex: And it’s all‑stock, so they’re not burning cash—just swapping equity for tech.

334
00:18:22,120 --> 00:18:26,860
Maya: The patch backlog they mentioned—tens of thousands—could be a real operational

335
00:18:26,860 --> 00:18:27,300
Maya: headache.

336
00:18:27,920 --> 00:18:32,680
Alex: I think the real test will be how quickly they can integrate those models into

337
00:18:32,680 --> 00:18:33,320
Alex: their platform.

338
00:18:33,960 --> 00:18:37,120
Maya: If they nail it, the next‑gen security numbers will keep climbing.

339
00:18:37,730 --> 00:18:40,930
Alex: Then it’s just a matter of scaling the new engine fast enough.

340
00:18:42,610 --> 00:18:45,100
Alex: Colette Kress just stepped in after Jensen Huang.

341
00:18:45,620 --> 00:18:49,020
Maya: Nadella said he trusts her, citing their long Microsoft ties.

342
00:18:49,590 --> 00:18:51,380
Alex: “All tragic, but I’ll give him space.”

343
00:18:51,950 --> 00:18:54,960
Maya: He also compared founders to gods, then said they die.

344
00:18:55,120 --> 00:18:56,040
Alex: No way, that’s a twist.

345
00:18:56,530 --> 00:18:57,980
Maya: He joked about Bill Gates too.

346
00:18:58,420 --> 00:19:00,840
Alex: So the tech big‑shot keeps the partnership steady.

347
00:19:01,400 --> 00:19:03,280
Maya: And that’s what matters to market watchers.

348
00:19:03,940 --> 00:19:06,280
Maya: What’s the deal with Colette’s background at Microsoft?

349
00:19:06,880 --> 00:19:10,260
Alex: She’s been on the cloud team for years, leads AI infra.

350
00:19:10,360 --> 00:19:11,620
Maya: So she knows Nvidia’s stack?

351
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:13,830
Alex: Not the tech, but the people.

352
00:19:13,830 --> 00:19:15,820
Alex: She worked with the same execs he did.

353
00:19:15,820 --> 00:19:16,980
Maya: That explains the trust.

354
00:19:17,680 --> 00:19:20,470
Alex: And Nadella said he’ll stay in touch with the same contacts.

355
00:19:21,100 --> 00:19:22,680
Maya: So it’s a people‑centric hand‑off.

356
00:19:23,120 --> 00:19:25,980
Alex: Yeah, it feels like a familiar hand pulling the wheel.

357
00:19:26,620 --> 00:19:29,480
Alex: Colette’s tech know‑how is shallow, but people are solid.

358
00:19:29,910 --> 00:19:32,560
Maya: The trust is purely people‑centric, not tech.

359
00:19:32,680 --> 00:19:33,760
Alex: No way, it’s a hand‑shake.

360
00:19:33,970 --> 00:19:35,000
Maya: So the partnership stays?

361
00:19:35,450 --> 00:19:37,220
Alex: It’s the same execs, same vibe.

362
00:19:37,220 --> 00:19:38,380
Maya: That’s the market’s comfort.

363
00:19:38,910 --> 00:19:40,680
Alex: Still, the disruption risk lingers.

364
00:19:41,180 --> 00:19:43,720
Maya: It’s a relief for investors, not a revelation.

365
00:19:45,370 --> 00:19:51,480
Maya: Humyn Labs just raised $47 million, and 90% of that cash is meant to feed U.S.

366
00:19:51,480 --> 00:19:52,780
Maya: robotics firms.

367
00:19:53,370 --> 00:19:58,180
Alex: So they’re buying data from people in their homes and turning it into AI training

368
00:19:58,180 --> 00:19:58,700
Alex: sets.

369
00:19:59,280 --> 00:20:05,170
Maya: Exactly, and the $26 million equity comes from Accel and a bunch of other names,

370
00:20:05,170 --> 00:20:08,440
Maya: plus $21 million in debt from Lighthouse Canton.

371
00:20:08,440 --> 00:20:09,440
Alex: That’s a lot of money.

372
00:20:09,930 --> 00:20:12,680
Maya: They’ve only got 12 paying customers,

373
00:20:12,680 --> 00:20:16,180
Maya: but already pulled in $10 million in revenue in half a year.

374
00:20:16,600 --> 00:20:21,120
Alex: And they’re paying workers daily while waiting 30–60 days for clients.

375
00:20:21,920 --> 00:20:23,940
Maya: That explains why the venture debt is a thing.

376
00:20:24,060 --> 00:20:24,260
Alex: Right.

377
00:20:25,280 --> 00:20:29,420
Maya: So the big surprise is the scale of the debt‑equity mix for a startup still

378
00:20:29,420 --> 00:20:30,900
Maya: hustling to get cash flow.

379
00:20:31,450 --> 00:20:33,500
Alex: What’s the trick behind their two‑camera setup?

380
00:20:33,860 --> 00:20:38,900
Maya: They’ve built a dual‑camera rig that captures people doing normal chores; then

381
00:20:38,900 --> 00:20:42,160
Maya: their software tags hand moves, object interactions,

382
00:20:42,160 --> 00:20:45,120
Maya: and turns the raw clips into labeled data.

383
00:20:45,127 --> 00:20:46,320
Alex: That’s a lot of detail.

384
00:20:46,830 --> 00:20:50,580
Maya: Yeah, and they run this across more than 20 countries, with Brazil,

385
00:20:50,580 --> 00:20:53,560
Maya: India and South Korea as the main hubs,

386
00:20:53,560 --> 00:20:56,820
Maya: pulling from a network that grew out of their earlier studio.

387
00:20:57,340 --> 00:20:59,400
Alex: So the founders came from a game studio?

388
00:20:59,740 --> 00:21:05,000
Maya: Right, Manish Agarwal was the former CEO of a gaming firm and Ishank Gupta had a

389
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:10,590
Maya: stint at AB InBev; they spun off Kratos Studios, now KGeN,

390
00:21:10,590 --> 00:21:13,500
Maya: which already had a contributor network that feeds Humyn Labs.

391
00:21:14,020 --> 00:21:15,660
Alex: That explains the data pipeline.

392
00:21:16,140 --> 00:21:20,730
Maya: And on the money side, they’ve hit $10 million in revenue in six months,

393
00:21:20,730 --> 00:21:26,170
Maya: with another $20 million in signed deals and another $20 million still pending,

394
00:21:26,170 --> 00:21:28,360
Maya: which is why the debt component is so prominent.

395
00:21:28,700 --> 00:21:29,520
Maya: That’s a heavy debt.

396
00:21:29,520 --> 00:21:30,640
Alex: Interesting debt mix.

397
00:21:31,240 --> 00:21:35,680
Maya: I think the debt shows they’re cash‑tight, but it also signals investor confidence.

398
00:21:36,240 --> 00:21:39,180
Alex: But the equity was huge, so maybe they’re just hedging.

399
00:21:39,620 --> 00:21:43,160
Maya: They only have 12 customers, so the revenue stream feels shaky.

400
00:21:43,280 --> 00:21:44,020
Alex: Huh, that’s a risk.

401
00:21:44,470 --> 00:21:46,480
Maya: My read is they’re betting on a data boom,

402
00:21:46,480 --> 00:21:51,600
Maya: but their model relies on daily payments to workers and long customer terms.

403
00:21:52,130 --> 00:21:55,620
Alex: It sounds like they’re building a bridge between labor and AI,

404
00:21:55,620 --> 00:21:59,140
Alex: but whether that bridge holds up remains to be seen.

405
00:22:00,780 --> 00:22:02,840
Alex: Hey Maya, did you see the IPO buzz?

406
00:22:03,420 --> 00:22:05,840
Maya: Yeah, Jaan Tallinn's stake could hit tens of billions.

407
00:22:06,620 --> 00:22:09,450
Alex: He’s the same guy who warned AI might end us.

408
00:22:10,200 --> 00:22:12,810
Maya: Right, he sold Skype, now betting on Anthropic.

409
00:22:13,350 --> 00:22:17,360
Alex: He invested 124 million before Claude even launched.

410
00:22:17,900 --> 00:22:21,200
Maya: Anthropic’s valuation could hit about 1.4 trillion.

411
00:22:21,420 --> 00:22:22,060
Alex: That’s insane.

412
00:22:22,870 --> 00:22:25,840
Maya: And he’s an early backer through effective altruism circles.

413
00:22:26,730 --> 00:22:28,860
Maya: What’s the real deal with his early stake, Alex?

414
00:22:29,410 --> 00:22:34,720
Alex: He put in $124 million in Series A, before Claude existed.

415
00:22:35,260 --> 00:22:36,920
Maya: He’s a doomer and a billionaire, huh?

416
00:22:37,260 --> 00:22:40,800
Alex: He sold Skype to eBay, then bet on safety‑first AI.

417
00:22:41,540 --> 00:22:43,730
Maya: Did he ever say why he’d back Anthropic?

418
00:22:44,140 --> 00:22:48,900
Alex: He said funding a safety‑conscious lab was the best way to keep a lid on risk.

419
00:22:49,060 --> 00:22:50,000
Maya: And he’s still holding?

420
00:22:50,430 --> 00:22:55,120
Alex: He’s one of the largest IPO winners; the return could be in the tens of billions.

421
00:22:55,840 --> 00:23:00,100
Alex: I think the IPO shows the market is still chasing hype, not safety.

422
00:23:00,660 --> 00:23:03,140
Maya: He’s betting on a lab that might not profit for years.

423
00:23:03,360 --> 00:23:04,060
Alex: I don’t buy it.

424
00:23:04,740 --> 00:23:06,900
Maya: But the valuation could make him a billionaire.

425
00:23:07,520 --> 00:23:09,860
Alex: It’s a gamble that could backfire if the tech stalls.

426
00:23:10,810 --> 00:23:13,200
Maya: Yet he’s a doomer, so maybe he’s hedging his bets.

427
00:23:13,810 --> 00:23:17,440
Alex: The real question is whether Anthropic can actually deliver.

428
00:23:17,910 --> 00:23:21,500
Maya: If they do, the return will be massive; if not, the hype will fade.

429
00:23:23,180 --> 00:23:29,720
Maya: Anthropic priced its IPO at $83, above the $70–78 range,

430
00:23:29,720 --> 00:23:31,960
Maya: valuing it at $1.5 trillion.

431
00:23:32,620 --> 00:23:33,600
Alex: 83 bucks?

432
00:23:33,600 --> 00:23:34,240
Alex: That’s a jump.

433
00:23:34,720 --> 00:23:38,500
Maya: The price was set after a strong demand wave, so investors dug in.

434
00:23:38,620 --> 00:23:39,140
Alex: Why the leap?

435
00:23:39,510 --> 00:23:44,580
Maya: The hype around AI still pulls in cash, even after Nvidia’s CEO exit.

436
00:23:44,580 --> 00:23:46,040
Alex: So it’s a hype‑cash combo.

437
00:23:46,870 --> 00:23:51,760
Maya: Amazon owns about 15%, Google almost 14%,

438
00:23:51,760 --> 00:23:56,060
Maya: and a bundle of tech firms bought $26 billion in non‑voting shares.

439
00:23:56,060 --> 00:23:57,360
Alex: Big names, big bucks.

440
00:23:58,060 --> 00:24:04,720
Maya: Co‑founders each sold 3.4 million shares, netting roughly $282 million each.

441
00:24:05,150 --> 00:24:08,020
Alex: What about the private placement—how big is that deal?

442
00:24:08,320 --> 00:24:12,220
Maya: They’re buying $26 billion of non‑voting shares,

443
00:24:12,220 --> 00:24:16,740
Maya: the same five tech giants that already own 15–14% each.

444
00:24:16,860 --> 00:24:17,720
Alex: 26 billion?

445
00:24:18,140 --> 00:24:20,920
Maya: Yes, that’s the figure that matched the IPO,

446
00:24:20,920 --> 00:24:23,340
Maya: and it’s a separate tranche outside the public offering.

447
00:24:23,800 --> 00:24:25,240
Alex: So the public is getting the rest.

448
00:24:26,235 --> 00:24:26,840
Maya: Exactly.

449
00:24:26,840 --> 00:24:32,360
Maya: And the founders, including Amodei, each pulled out 3.4 million shares,

450
00:24:32,360 --> 00:24:35,600
Maya: cashing in around $282 million apiece.

451
00:24:35,860 --> 00:24:36,780
Alex: That’s a tidy exit.

452
00:24:37,160 --> 00:24:41,300
